  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather continues today, though a few very\nisolated instability/sea breeze showers will occur this afternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure ridges across the area today before moving offshore\ntonight. The high is relatively weak and with the warm and humid\nairmass, there could be a few pop-up showers across the sea-breeze\nfronts today. Coverage on Friday was rather sparse, and it appears\nit will be that way again today. We'll keep the 10%-20% POP that\nthe NBM offered across Delaware and up across North NJ. Highs\ntemps will again reach the mid/upper 80s most spots and few low\n90s are expected too. Cooler readings for the shore and southern\nPoconos. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms increase Sunday night and\nMonday with the potential for areas of heavy rain with some flash\nflooding risk.\n\nAn upper-level trough will shift across eastern Canada this\nweekend and early next week. While this occurs, strong energy \ncrests a ridge in the West and this carves the trough out more \ninto the Tennessee and Ohio valleys by later this weekend. This \nmay result in the trough becoming closed off into early next \nweek as it gradually approaches the East. The main trough may \ndetach and depart to the northeast leaving behind a lingering \nclosed low. This scenario would lead to the system slowing its \neastward progression through the first half of next week. The \nmodel trends continue to slow the arrival of this system, with \nthe bulk of the showers and thunderstorms occurring later Sunday\nnight and especially on Monday.\n\nAhead of the strengthening upper-level trough, deep south to southwest\nflow will increase and this will ramp up the low-level moisture and\ntemperature advection. It will therefore be a humid time frame as\nthe precipitable waters surge to 2+ inches. There may be multiple\nrounds of showers and thunderstorms, starting with an initial \none later Sunday night into Monday with the increased warm air \nadvection regime. Instability will increase, however the timing \nat night should limit the thunderstorm intensity especially \nsince it may be more of elevated convection initially. Areas of \nheavy rain however will be probable given the increasing forcing\nfor ascent and high water content air. The WPC has included much\nof our region in a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall for\nSunday night into Monday. On Monday, ripples of energy along an\nincoming cold front during may enhance the convective potential\nand with deep southerly flow, some training convection will be \npossible. Excessive rainfall rates would enhance the flash \nflooding risk, especially from the I-95 corridor to the north \nand west where the elevation rise could boost the ascent some \ngiven a southeast to south low-level flow. There is a Slight \nRisk (level 2 out of 4) for Excessive rainfall exists for Monday\nand Monday night for most of our eastern Pennsylvania counties,\nmost of New Jersey and into northern Delaware. There is a Marginal\nRisk (level 1 out of 4) for the rest of our area. If enough heating\ndevelops during the course of Monday, that combined with some increase\nin shear (some initially backed low- level flow) could lead to at\nleast some severe thunderstorm risk. This potential is less certain\nthough given the chance for multiple rounds of convection/cloud\ncover, and a moist atmospheric profile which tends to limit the\noverall instability.\n\nGiven the slow eastward progression of this system, the chances for \nconvection look to continue for multiple days next week as the cold \nfront may stall in our vicinity. The current forecast does have shower\nand thunderstorm chances each day next week, mainly in the afternoon\nand evening. The most active day, as of now, however looks to be\nMonday given the overlap of favorable parameters for more widespread\nconvective development.",
